<BaseLayout
  title="Children's Church — Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? | Nazaryah"
  description="Every church says the family comes first, then sends the children down the hall. From Raikes to Rockefeller to the youth room, the record of who took the teaching of the children, and why Pharaoh wanted the little ones left behind."
>
  <article class="cod-article">
    <div class="cod-article-inner">
      <a href="/christian-or-demonic" class="cod-back"><span class="arrow">←</span><span>Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</span></a>

      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Seven · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">Children's Church</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">Go Now, Ye That Are Men</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Every church says the family comes first. Then it sends the children down the hall. Pharaoh made the same offer to Moses.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Introduction</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">Sunday morning. A family walks through the church doors together, and within two minutes it is taken apart. The baby goes to the nursery. The six-year-old goes to children's church, with puppets, snacks, and a video. The fourteen-year-old goes to the youth room, where the music is louder, the lights are lower, and a youth pastor in his twenties will teach him what to believe. The mother and father walk into the sanctuary alone and sit through a service their children will never hear.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Ask the pastor about it, and he will tell you the family is the foundation. Many churches print it in their statement of belief: parents are the primary teachers of their children. He means it. Then the church builds a system in which the parent never teaches his child anything, because someone down the hall is paid to do it.</p>
      <p class="cod-body"><strong>That is a church practice, not a doctrine.</strong> No church votes on it. It simply is the way things are done, so no one asks the question this series asks of everything: <strong>where did it come from?</strong> In Scripture, the children stood in the assembly with their parents and heard the Word read aloud. Separating them by age, with their own rooms, their own teachers, their own music, and their own culture, came later — and the record of how it came is open. Follow it. Then ask whether it comes from the Spirit of truth, or follows a pattern the enemy has used on Yahuah's people from the beginning.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part One</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">A School for Children With No One</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">The first Sunday school was not a church program. In 1780, in the English city of Gloucester, a newspaper publisher named Robert Raikes looked at the children of the poor. Most of them worked six days a week in the mills and factories. On Sunday, their one day off, they ran wild in the streets. No one taught them anything. Many of their parents could not read either.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Raikes hired women to gather these children on Sunday and teach them to read, using the Bible as the reading book. He wrote about it in his newspaper, the idea spread, and within fifty years over a million children in Britain were attending Sunday schools. It was a rescue: a charity for children who had no one at home to teach them.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Then it crossed the ocean and moved into the churches. By the late 1800s the Sunday school was no longer for the children of the street. It was for every child in the congregation, held in its own rooms, divided by age, with printed lessons and its own teachers. The father who used to teach his own children now dropped them at a classroom door.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Spirit of Truth?</strong> Caring for the child with no father is the heart of Yahuah's Law. He is "a father of the fatherless" (Psalm 68:5), and the Law commands that the fatherless be fed and protected (Deuteronomy 14:29). Raikes's first schools did that.</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or Seducing Spirits?</strong> Watch what happened to it. A remedy built for children with no father became the standard for children who had one. The father was relieved of the one job Yahuah gave him by name: "thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children" (Deuteronomy 6:7). The enemy did not have to forbid fathers to teach. He only had to give them a reason not to. <strong>A practice that turns every child into a fatherless child, one Sunday at a time, is not serving the fatherless. It is making more of them.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Two</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Whoever Shapes the Child</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">At the same time, the governments of the world were learning what the church was about to learn: whoever teaches the children shapes the nation.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Prussia led the way. In 1763 King Frederick the Great ordered every child in the countryside into school from age five to thirteen. After Prussia's defeat by Napoleon, the philosopher Johann Fichte gave a series of addresses to the German nation (1807-1808) calling for a new state education. He said plainly what such schooling was for: the student must be fashioned so that "he simply cannot will otherwise than what you wish him to will."</p>
      <p class="cod-body">In 1843 the American school reformer Horace Mann traveled to Prussia, came home full of praise for its schools, and urged Massachusetts to follow. In 1852 Massachusetts passed the first compulsory school attendance law in the United States. Other states followed, until every child in the country was required to spend his days in a classroom under a teacher chosen by someone other than his father.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Then came the money. In 1902 John D. Rockefeller funded the General Education Board, which poured millions into American schools, especially in the rural South. Its chairman, Frederick Gates, described the board's vision in 1913 in a paper called <em>The Country School of To-Morrow</em>: "In our dream, we have limitless resources, and the people yield themselves with perfect docility to our molding hand." Gates went on to say the aim was not to raise up scholars, writers, or professionals from the farm families, but to train their children for a good life right where they were. Readers can judge the dream for themselves. The words "our molding hand" are his.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Spirit of Truth?</strong> Scripture has already named the molding hand, and it is not a board of trustees. Yahuah sent Jeremiah down to the potter's house to watch the clay turn on the wheel, and then said, "as the clay is in the potter's hand, so are ye in mine hand" (Jeremiah 18:6). Isaiah answered for the people: "we are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand" (Isaiah 64:8). Under that hand, Yahuah gave the shaping of a child to the father.</p>
        <p class="cod-body">In Scripture the molding hand belongs to the father, under Yahuah. "Train up a child in the way he should go" (Proverbs 22:6) is spoken to parents. Yahuah "established a testimony in Jacob, and appointed a law in Israel, which he commanded our fathers, that they should make them known to their children" (Psalm 78:5).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or Seducing Spirits?</strong> Every power in Scripture that wanted to own Yahuah's people went for the children first. Pharaoh ordered the Hebrew boys killed (Exodus 1:22). Herod did the same at Bethlehem (Matthew 2:16). Babylon took a gentler road. Nebuchadnezzar chose the brightest young men of Judah, gave them three years of Babylon's learning and language, fed them from the king's table, and changed their names (Daniel 1:3-7). Daniel, "El is my judge," became Belteshazzar, named for Babylon's god. Hananiah, "Yah is gracious," and Azariah, "Yah has helped," lost the name of Yah altogether. <strong>Babylon did not need to kill the children. It only needed to teach them.</strong> That is the same goal Fichte wrote down in plain words: a child who cannot will otherwise. And when a man dreams of a people that "yield themselves with perfect docility to our molding hand," he is reaching for the potter's place. Isaiah warned about exactly that: "shall the work say of him that made it, He made me not?" (Isaiah 29:16), and "Woe unto him that striveth with his Maker!" (Isaiah 45:9). <strong>Only one Potter has the right to the clay.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Three</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Youth Movement</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">The church soon gave its teenagers their own world too. In 1881 a Congregational minister in Portland, Maine, named Francis Clark founded the Christian Endeavor Society, a meeting just for young people. It spread fast. Within a few years there were hundreds of societies, and the denominations built their own versions — the Methodist Epworth League, the Lutheran Luther League.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">The great leap came in the 1940s. In 1941 Jim Rayburn founded Young Life, built on reaching high schoolers outside the church through clubs, camps, and fun. In 1944 Youth for Christ began holding Saturday night rallies for teenagers with lively music, entertainers, and a sermon, under the slogan "Geared to the times, anchored to the Rock." Its first full-time evangelist was a young preacher named Billy Graham. Youth culture now had its own church.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">The same years saw the other side of the world run the same idea to its end. In Germany, the Hitler Youth, founded in 1926, became the state's official youth organization in 1936, and membership was made compulsory. Hitler explained why in a speech in November 1933, as recorded by the historian William Shirer. When a parent told him he would never join the movement, Hitler said he answered calmly: "Your child belongs to us already." The parent would pass away, he said, but the children were already in the new camp, "and in a short time they will know nothing else."</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Young Life and Youth for Christ were not the Hitler Youth, and nothing in this record says they copied it. They were sincere men trying to reach teenagers. But the same lesson was being learned on both sides of the ocean in the same decade: <strong>gather the young apart from their parents, give them their own music and their own leaders and their own world, and in one generation they will know nothing else.</strong></p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Spirit of Truth?</strong> When mothers brought their little ones to Yahushua, it was His disciples who tried to send the children away — and Yahushua "was much displeased," and said, "Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not" (Mark 10:13-14). He did not send them to another room. He took them up in His arms in front of everyone (Mark 10:16).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or Seducing Spirits?</strong> Look again at Babylon's method in Daniel 1. Separate the young. Give them their own teachers. Give them their own food from the king's table. Give them a new culture and a new name. Daniel's answer was to refuse the king's table (Daniel 1:8). The youth room repeats the method: its own pastor, its own music, its own culture, its own language, built to feel nothing like the assembly the parents sit in. Paul named what the last days would bring: men "disobedient to parents" (2 Timothy 3:2). <strong>The enemy does not need children to hate their fathers. He only needs them to grow up in a different room.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Four</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Children's Church</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">After World War II, the baby boom filled the churches with children, and the churches built for them. New buildings came with education wings, nurseries, and classrooms graded by age. In the following decades, more and more churches stopped letting the youngest children sit through the service at all. During the sermon they were led out to "children's church," a service of their own with songs, games, crafts, and a short lesson. The paid youth pastor became a normal staff position. By the end of the century, a child could attend church every week from birth to high school graduation without once sitting in the assembly with his father.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Then the children left. Surveys of American churches over the last twenty years have found the same thing again and again: about two out of three young people who attended church as teenagers stop attending for at least a year after high school. Many never come back. They had never belonged to the assembly. They had belonged to the youth group — and the youth group ended at graduation.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Spirit of Truth?</strong> In Scripture the children were in the room. When the Law was read every seven years, Moses commanded, "Gather the people together, men, and women, and children … that they may hear, and that they may learn" (Deuteronomy 31:12). Joshua read all the Law "before all the congregation of Israel, with the women, and the little ones" (Joshua 8:35). Joel called the solemn assembly to "gather the children, and those that suck the breasts" (Joel 2:16). And when Paul's letter to Ephesus was read aloud to the assembly, it spoke straight to the children sitting there: "Children, obey your parents in the Master" (Ephesians 6:1).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or Seducing Spirits?</strong> When Pharaoh finally agreed to let Israel go and worship, he made one offer. "Go now ye that are men, and serve Yahuah" (Exodus 10:11). Worship all you like — but leave the little ones behind. Moses refused: "We will go with our young and with our old, with our sons and with our daughters" (Exodus 10:9). <strong>Pharaoh's offer is the floor plan of the modern church. The men go into the sanctuary. The little ones stay behind, down the hall.</strong> Moses would not leave Egypt without them. The church walks into worship every Sunday and leaves them with someone else.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Five</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">Yahuah did not leave the teaching of children to chance, or to a program. He built it into the life of the home and the feasts.</p>
      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>At home, all day.</strong> "These words … shall be in thine heart: and thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children, and shalt talk of them when thou sittest in thine house, and when thou walkest by the way, and when thou liest down, and when thou risest up" (Deuteronomy 6:6-7).</li>
        <li><strong>At the feasts, by question.</strong> Yahuah built Pesach so the child would ask: "when your children shall say unto you, What mean ye by this service?" — and the father answers (Exodus 12:26-27). The feast is a lesson designed to be taught by a father at his own table.</li>
        <li><strong>From one generation to the next.</strong> Fathers were to teach their children, "that the generation to come might know them, even the children which should be born; who should arise and declare them to their children: that they might set their hope in God … and keep his commandments" (Psalm 78:6-7).</li>
        <li><strong>In the apostles' day, the same.</strong> Paul gave the job to fathers: "bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Master" (Ephesians 6:4). Timothy knew the Scriptures "from a child" because his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice taught him (2 Timothy 1:5; 3:15).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="cod-body">Notice what all of these have in common. The teacher is the parent. The classroom is the home, the road, and the feast. The textbook is the Law of Yahuah. And the goal is not a fun morning but a child who will "keep his commandments" and teach them to his own children after him.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Six</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">So what? The children are learning Bible stories down the hall. Isn't that better than nothing? That is how most parents see it. But Paul said the real fight is "against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world" (Ephesians 6:12), and the enemy has always known where the future of a people lies.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">The last words of the Old Testament are about exactly this. Yahuah promised to send Elijah to "turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse" (Malachi 4:6). The turning of fathers and children toward each other is what stands between the land and the curse. A system that turns them away from each other, every week, works against the very thing Malachi said must happen.</p>
      <p class="cod-pullquote">The enemy does not need to steal the children. He only needs the fathers to hand them over.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Seven</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">Children's church is not a doctrine. It is a practice — a church thing — and that is why no one questions it. Every church says the family comes first. Then the church takes the teaching of the children away from the family and gives it to a program.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Look at the root. A charity school for children with no father, turned into the standard for children who had one. A state school system built on the Prussian goal of shaping a child who cannot will otherwise, and funded by men who dreamed of a "molding hand." A youth movement that gave teenagers their own world in the same decade a dictator told parents their children already belonged to him. And a children's church that sends the little ones out of the room while the Word is read. Every piece of it moves the child further from his father, and every piece came after the apostles.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Scripture asks for the opposite: a father who teaches at his own table, a child who asks at the feast, and a whole family standing together when the Word is read. That is not a program. It is a home.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Revelation shows what the enemy has always been after. When the woman was about to give birth, "the dragon stood before the woman … for to devour her child as soon as it was born" (Revelation 12:4). He failed. So he went "to make war with the remnant of her seed, which keep the commandments of God, and have the testimony of Yahushua Messiah" (Revelation 12:17). His war has always been against the children — and the children who keep the commandments are the ones he hates most.</p>
      <p class="cod-pullquote">Pharaoh said, leave the little ones. Moses said, we go together. Every Sunday, the church has to choose which one it will answer.</p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

